## What happened

Apple removed DSA support from its security frameworks. .NET on macOS delegates to these native APIs, so starting in .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Windows and Linux are unaffected (Windows uses CNG; Linux uses OpenSSL, both still support DSA).

## How to fix it

Since you only need **verification** of legacy signatures, you have two practical options:

### Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended)
Replace the platform-dependent `System.Security.Cryptography.DSA` with **BouncyCastle**, which is a managed (pure C#) implementation that works everywhere:

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] data, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

### Option 2 — Runtime guard (short-term)
If you want to keep using the built-in API on platforms that support it:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
else
{
    // fall back to BouncyCastle or skip
}
```

### Recommendation

Go with **Option 1**. DSA is a legacy algorithm — a managed cross-platform implementation avoids future breakage on any OS. If possible, plan a longer-term migration to **Ed25519** or **ECDSA** for new signatures.
